A||||20||DJRice||This one is killing me!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||DJRice||04-16-2001||08:09 AM||||I used to think I understood something about these cars, but I have to wonder about that.  The car is a 1986 SE V6 and the problem is oil pressure.  Basically, the oil pressure would get low and sometimes at idle drop below 20psi thus engaging the fuel pump shut off.  <P>The obvious solution is to replace the oil pump.  So we did.  Now, with the brand new oil pump the problem remains.  <P>So I thought that the pressure sender may just be hokey so I replaced that.<P>No Joy.  <P>I also blew out the pressure sender feed tube with the air compressor, which did absolutely nothing.  <P>So basically, (after 1 oil pump, 2 pressure senders, 10 quarts of oil, and a mechanical gauge) what I have is an intermittent low oil pressure problem.  I dont lose oil pressure completely, because the mechanical gauge shows that the pressure bottoms out at about 18psi.  Just low enough to kill the fuel pump.  (which is really a shame because the engine runs smooth and strong)<P>I am so tired of this problem it's not even funny.  This is the kind of crap that gets 3.4L motors installed in cars.  <P>Do I pull the oil pump and try a new one?  Perhaps a high volume this time?<P>Do I try some of that oil system cleaner stuff?<P>Someone please tell me what I've forgotten to do to make this work.  I can't stand it any more.<P>:|<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<P>"Who wants somma Debo?"||12.13.226.12||reg||8
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||04-16-2001||09:04 AM||||I dont know if this engine has an oil pressure relief valve, something that keeps the pressure from getting too high, if so it might be getting stuck open.<P>Low oil pressure at idle is one sign of worn main bearings on an engine. How many miles on the block? Have you ever run the oil dry, had the pressure drop to zero? If you have very worn bearings, or if you spun a bearing, the oil pressure will drop when the engine is hot and idling.<P>I am only speaking from a general knowledge of car engines, not from specific GM/Fiero engine knowledge (which I dont have). There may be another explaination for your pressure problem.<P>If it is due to worn bearings then a high volume oil pump will fix it for a while, but the bearings will need to be replaced soon. Once they start getting loose they wear even faster because of the play between the moving parts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||peterh||04-16-2001||10:55 AM||||For a quick fix put in heavier oil, 10/40w with 1 or 2 qts of 20/50w. My oil pressure would go into red with 10/30w but is at 20lbs at idle with the thicker oil. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-16-2001||11:11 AM||||With 85,000 miles on my car, if I put is 5W-30 oil pressure at idle is low. I run 10W-40 and oil pressure is fine. As engine's get more miles on them it is better to go to 10W-40 or even 20W-50 depending on the wear. 5W-30 is good for new/rebuilt engines where the tolorence's of everything is good. Generally I use 10W-40 in all my cars that are not overhead cam. (Overhead cam engines have smaller tolorence's)<P>Don't use Engine oil cleaners! On older engines if you put in a cleaner you usually end up with alot of oil leaks. There are many oil additives on the market, in my opinion none of them work. The Marvel Mystery oil is the only thing I would recommend putting into the engine other than a good oil. Duralube, slick50, motor-up, prolong, etc are just placibo's. All those infomercials are staged. If I remember correctly Slick50 has a lawsuit against them for their false claims. Synthetic oils are basically the same as regular oil in terms of lubrication. It does seem that the synthetic oils trap deposits better and cut down on the acidity of the deposits. (The acidity of the deposits is what causes the most bearing wear) Someone posted a website that ran down all the "truth's" of oils for vehicles. The above is a brief run-down of what it says.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||04-16-2001||11:51 AM||||I thought the fuel pump cut-off wasn't until oil pressure dropped below 4 psi?||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||04-16-2001||02:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B><P>Low oil pressure at idle is one sign of worn main bearings on an engine. How many miles on the block? Have you ever run the oil dry, had the pressure drop to zero? If you have very worn bearings, or if you spun a bearing, the oil pressure will drop when the engine is hot and idling.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I tend to agree I did an engine rebuild and got 1 of the cam bearings in just a bit crooked and the result was very low oil pressure EVEN with a high volume oil pump. If the engine went dry at anytime in its life (even doing routine oil changes)chances are very high that a cam bearing suffered. <P>I always pull the fuel pump fuse and crank the oil up to pressure w/o starting the engine to prevent this.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT GT 87||04-16-2001||02:22 PM||||I bought a 87 GT a year ago with a slight rod knock. 5w30 -it would knock good..  Changed to Mobil 1   20w50  And it almost went away ---------Better across the board oil press.  too!||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||04-16-2001||02:23 PM||||cant something be rigged up to bypass the fuel shutoff like running the hot wire direct to fusebox?||||reg||1
Z||000008||fotofrank||04-16-2001||02:49 PM||||I put rally gauges and a V6 oil pressure sensor in my '84 Duke. The oil in the car now<BR>is, I belive, 10W-30W. Oil pressure at idal is about 37lbs and 40lbs at 20 - 70 mph.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||4
Z||000009||DJRice||04-16-2001||02:57 PM||||The car has less that 100k miles on the original motor, and to my knowledge has never been run dry.  We used 5 quarts of 20w50 when we filled it up both times.  <P>I tried bypassing the cutoff and all I managed to do was hotwire the fuel pump so it was on all the time. <P>If the pressure cutoff is supposed to be at 4psi then I might have some sort of electrical problem because it never goes that low before it starts to die off.    <P>As for the main bearings, I guess when you have eliminated everything else, what youve got left must be the problem....Thats kinda crappy but I guess its the case.   <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<P>"Who wants somma Debo?"||||reg||1
Z||000010||thomas_l||04-16-2001||03:27 PM||||I just got my 166k mile 87 GT on the road with it's new tranny and discovered the engine was a little "loose". Just a little bearing chatter up near the redline and low oil pressure/warning lamp at idle. I dropped in a $2 bottle of motor honey in there and the light flickers for an instant at low idle and less bearing chatter at higher revs. <P>The fuel pump oil pressure switch is meant to bypass a failed fuel pump relay, not to kill fuel on low pressure. I haven't looked at the diagram in some years, but I believe the circuit through that oil pressure switch is parallel to the relay circuit (i.e. redundant). If the relay fails, you would have extended cranking until the O/P switch closed and powers up the fuel pump. Check your fuel pump relay and it's circuits.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fierowrecker||04-16-2001||06:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DJRice:<BR><B>The car has less that 100k miles on the original motor, and to my knowledge has never been run dry.  We used 5 quarts of 20w50 when we filled it up both times.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You have 20W50 and it drops below 20 PSI????<BR>Time to open it up and see what is coming apart before it blows!!!<BR>I have torn down 2 2.5L with roller lifters, and the lifters had come apart, BIG drop in pressure...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000012||grinthock||04-16-2001||06:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>cant something be rigged up to bypass the fuel shutoff like running the hot wire direct to fusebox?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah the relay and conenctions for the fuel pump are in front of the Air can (if you still have one) just short the oil pump lead<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rodrv6||04-16-2001||08:28 PM||||There seems to be a misconception that the oil pressure switch is a fuel pump cut off in the event of low oil pressure. NOT TRUE!!!The oil pressure switch is there to act as a back up to the fuel pump relay.  In case the relay dies for some reason, the oil pressure switch will keep the pump running as long as there is oil pressure.  If your pump is quitting because of low oil pressure, then you also have a fuel pump relay problem.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||4
Z||000014||bubbajoexx||04-16-2001||08:59 PM||||the fuel pump relay is for initial startup for when the computer signals the pump for 3 sec. to load the fuel system at start up the oil pressure switch takes over for the relay which cuts out if the relay were to stay in the point would burn out . if you have low oil pressure it means it time to rebuild the bottom end bearing rod and mains oil is spilling past they hence low oil pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000015||p4n1c||04-16-2001||09:25 PM||||We had the same problem with oil pressure whenever the Cutlass would idle, it would be normal for a while and then all of sudden drop to nothing, dad would have to drop it into neutral and hold the revs up to keep the pressure up.  <P>The problem was the oil sensors.  The 2.8 in the Cutlass had two sensors and they were shot.  My dad put on a new oil pump at the request of the mechanic and no go, so he had a hunch and replaced the oil sensors and it works fine now.<P>Worth a shot if this car even has sensors for the oil.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)||||reg||1
Z||000016||87GTZ34||04-16-2001||11:12 PM||||What Rod said.  I had the same problem w/ my 1st GT.  I took it for a ride (1st drive after I brought it home), went 1/4 mi made a u turn and coasted to the side of the road.  Waited 10 min for the oil to cool/thicken and restarted.  Then I drove 2 footed keeping the revs at about 1K.  If you have your car off and turn the key to the run position, you should hear the fuel pump kick on for 2 seconds (or something like that). If that doesn't happen then you main fuel pump circuit has a problem and my problem was the relay. The oil sender is the backup circuit.  This is found on page 6E3-C2-5 in the 1987 Pontiac Fiero manual.<BR>  I think they did the backup circuit because they put the relay above the cat near where hot air is to exit the engine bay. That would be the hottest part under the decklid...||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||04-17-2001||07:51 AM||||Well, it sounds like although I did not understand how the oil pressure sender and fuel pump worked together, if the oil pressure drops the fuel pump gets cut off.    <P>Of course, the crappy thing is it sounds like a(some) main bearing(s) are in not good shape.  Time to yank the engine...again.  The thing had an exhaust leak so we pulled the whole motor last weekend to fix that and the oil pressure problem.  <P>Looks like I learned something new. <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000018||Rodrv6||04-17-2001||03:56 PM||||I hate to disagree with bubbajoexx, but the oil pressure switch does NOT "take over" from the fuel pump relay after the engine starts.  I've tried this same test on my 84 4 cylinder and my 88 GT.  With the engine running, disconnect the plug from the oil pressure sending unit.  If all is well with the fuel pump relay, the car will continue to run.  If the car quits, the fuel pump relay circuit has a problem.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000019||DJRice||04-17-2001||04:09 PM||||Hmm...Well I pulled the plug on the oil pressure sender and the gauge floated high but the engine kept running.  I believe that the engine shut off when I grounded the gauge lead but cant test it because the car is at my parents house.  I'll have to call my dad to have him verify that.  <P>All that having been said, if I had spun a bearing or something, you'd think that I would know it by the sound, but I dont hear anything out of the ordinary......<P>Unbelievable....At this point, maybe we should just replace the mains for the heck of it.  Weve done everything else.  Argh.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000020||DJRice||04-17-2001||06:43 PM||||Well, I talked to my dad earlier and it looks like it was indeed the mains.  <P>Thanks to all for the discussion.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||9
